# ── .env.example ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The names of the build-time values your app reads, without their values.
# Copy this file to .env and fill it in locally; .env is gitignored and must
# never be committed.
#
# Add a line here (name only) whenever the app starts reading a value, for
# example a server URL or a public client key. Server secrets, passwords and
# private keys never belong in the app: anything inside the app package can be
# read by whoever installs it.
#
# Values are passed at build time, never bundled as an asset (so do not load a
# .env file at runtime with a package):
#   flutter run --dart-define-from-file=.env
# and read in Dart as compile-time constants:
#   const value = String.fromEnvironment('NAME');
#
# The two environments are staging and production. The build workflow
# (build.yml) passes --dart-define=APP_ENV=<environment> to every build, and
# adds --dart-define-from-file=env/<environment>.json when that file exists, so
# keep the non-secret values that differ per environment (for example the API
# base URL) in env/staging.json and env/production.json.
#
# In CI, store real secrets as environment secrets, never in the repository
# (docs/github-setup.md, section 3).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
